"comparison": "Both Microsoft and JPMorgan Chase describe a hierarchical governance structure for cybersecurity where specific management committees oversee implementation and controls, with escalation paths to broader risk committees. Microsoft identifies 'dedicated management-level cybersecurity risk committees' that monitor risks, with significant risks escalated to the 'Non-Financial Risk Committee' (ms_2024_7_P399_S001, ms_2024_7_P399_S003). JPMorgan Chase identifies the 'Cybersecurity and Technology Controls Operating Committee (CTOC)' as the 'principal management committee' overseeing cybersecurity risk and controls, which 'escalates key operational risk and control issues... to the Global Technology Operating Committee' (jpm_2024_7_P748_S001, jpm_2024_7_P749_S001). Both companies rely on principal management-level committees for day-to-day oversight with formal escalation mechanisms.",
